{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# RE-SCOPED against the limit's stated PURPOSE — and the flat forced landing is now WITHDRAWN\n\nDesign intent supplied by the operator: **the step budget exists to bound FAILURES (observed runaway\ntool calls under error conditions), NOT to cap capacity. It was explicitly never meant to restrict\ntool calls for large epics.** That inverts my previous plan, which proposed a flat forced landing at\n~239 requests. I ran three more live gate runs to test the alternatives against that intent.\n\n## Four REAL runs of this gate, same codebase state (except bf31's, which predates its own fix)\n\n| run | criteria | calls | **distinct** | redundant | distinct ratio | outcome | `w24 distinct<=0.5` fires |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| FULL | 14 | 258 | **238** | 20 | **0.922** | died empty | **never** |\n| SPLIT-A | 7 | 257 | **76** | **181** | 0.296 | died empty | **call 88** |\n| SPLIT-B | 7 | 270 | 167 | 103 | 0.619 | died empty | **call 196** |\n| bf31 pre-fix | 14 | 264 | 135 | 129 | 0.511 | died empty | **call 151** |\n\n**THREE OF FOUR RUNS LOOPED.** Only the full post-bf31 run was clean. Repetition wasted **40-70% of\nevery pathological run** (181, 103 and 129 redundant calls). SPLIT-A's loop is textbook: the\nIDENTICAL call `search_files(\"_loader\", \"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler\")` repeated **144 times**,\nreturning the identical 12,554-char result each time, from call #81 to the end. SPLIT-B looped 62x on\n`search_files(\"deleted\", tests/.../test_backend_characterization.py)` -> an **honest** `(no matches)`\nthat the agent simply would not accept.\n\nSo the runaway-under-error-conditions failure the budget was built to bound is **the dominant,\nreproducible failure of this gate today, and it is currently unbounded**. That is the thing to fix.\n\n## WITHDRAWN: the flat forced landing at ~239 requests\n\nIt contradicts the stated intent and the data refutes its necessity. The ONLY run that was genuinely\nbudget-bound by *breadth* is the clean FULL run — 92% distinct, 93.8-100% novelty in its final block.\nCutting that off at 239 requests would land a verdict on deliberately-truncated evidence for\nlegitimate work: a worse verdict, not a safer one. Dropped.\n\n## PRIMARY FIX: wire an actuator to a repetition discriminator that actually works\n\n### The existing detector's headline metric is BROKEN — measured, filed as [rebar:70bc-ab05-6d4c-45a0]\n\n`max_consecutive_repeat` (`src/rebar/llm/usage_log.py:213-235`) counts only *adjacent identical*\nsignatures, so it is blind to any cycle of length >= 2:\n\n| trace | what it is | `max_consecutive_repeat` |\n|---|---|---|\n| bf31 pre-fix | a genuine **4-call cycle** repeated 31-37x | **1** |\n| FULL (healthy) | genuine breadth | **5** |\n\nThe pathological run scores **below** the healthy one. This is precisely why\n[rebar:a89d-4dd2-f421-417a] measured `max_consecutive_repeat=1` and concluded *\"It is NOT a runaway\nloop\"*. **An actuator wired to this metric would fire on healthy work and miss real cycles.** It\ncatches SPLIT-A's 1-cycle (137) only because that degenerate case is the one shape it can see.\n\n### The discriminator that does work: a windowed distinct-ratio\n\nLowest distinct-ratio over any trailing window, across all four runs:\n\n| window | bf31 | SPLIT-A | SPLIT-B | **FULL (healthy)** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 16 | 0.250 | **0.062** | 0.062 | **0.750** |\n| 24 | 0.167 | **0.042** | — | **0.833** |\n| 32 | 0.125 | **0.031** | — | **0.875** |\n\nA rule `distinct(last 24 tool calls) <= 0.50` fires on **all three** pathological runs (calls 151, 88,\n196) and **never** on the healthy one, with a ~5x margin. It is also **order-insensitive** (a set\ncardinality over a window), so unlike adjacency it is robust to tool calls completing out of emission\norder — which matters, because these traces log completion order.\n\n### Feasibility — confirmed, and it needs no new detector\n\n* `run_ctx.messages` **is** available inside the tool-filter predicate (verified against\n  `pydantic_ai.tools.RunContext` fields), alongside `run_ctx.usage.tool_calls`.\n* `usage_log.failure_usage` duck-types on a plain message list (`type(m).__name__ == \"ModelResponse\"`,\n  `\"ToolCallPart\"`), so **a89d's signature logic runs verbatim on the live history** — the remedy is\n  literally wiring the existing detector to act, once [rebar:70bc-ab05-6d4c-45a0] gives it a signal\n  that separates cycles from breadth.\n* The actuator is the **already-shipped** mechanism: withdraw tools (`agent_call.py:64-73`) so the\n  agent must land a verdict on the evidence it has, instead of being killed with nothing.\n\n### The `tool_calls_limit` path is still covered — as a FAILURE bound, not a workload ceiling\n\n`build_usage_limits` (`structured_run.py:399-412`) installs a second, independent kill path:\n`tool_calls_limit = max(8, eff_max_iter)` = 480 vs `request_limit` 240. `run_step` does not bound it.\nMeasured: at 3 tool calls/request a `tool_step_limit` of 239, 216 **and** 180 all still die; my clean\nrun sat at 258/240 = 1.075 and a89d measured a real run at 475/240 = **1.979** — clearing 480 by 2\ncalls. Because the discriminator is computed over **tool calls**, a runaway that burns calls without\nburning requests trips it on the same signal. No separate workload ceiling is introduced.\n(Enforcement point established from the vendored source: `UsageLimits.check_before_tool_call`,\n`pydantic_ai/usage.py:412-419`, called at `_agent_graph.py:1703-1706` with **projected** usage — it\nraises *before* executing, so the guard must act while headroom remains.)\n\n## The AC-split fallback — TESTED LIVE, and it is NOT sufficient\n\nSplit BY EXPLICIT AC INDEX (never inferred), each half carrying the **full** context: 14 criteria ->\n7 + 7, context 99,347 -> 96,631 (A) / 96,086 (B) chars. The criteria set **does** partition cleanly —\neach is an independent `- [x]` item, so this is mechanically sound.\n\n**Both halves still died empty.** And the workload benefit is unproven:\n\n| run | criteria | distinct calls | **distinct per criterion** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| FULL | 14 | 238 | 17.0 |\n| SPLIT-A | 7 | 76 | 10.9 |\n| SPLIT-B | 7 | 167 | **23.9** |\n\nSPLIT-B spent **more** evidence per criterion than the full run. Per-criterion cost varies 10.9-23.9\nacross runs, so run-to-run variance dominates any halving effect — on this evidence the split does not\nreliably bring a run inside the budget. Verdict combination (FAIL in either half => FAIL overall) is\nstraightforward, but two signatures over two disjoint criteria subsets is a **material change to what\nthe attestation means** and needs its own design. **The split is a capability, not this bug's fix.**\nIt should be its own ticket if wanted — and note it is only viable at all *with* the repetition\nactuator, since both halves were killed by loops, not by breadth.\n\n## Honest statement of what this fix delivers\n\n**It does NOT make e369's full-criteria close reach a verdict.** The clean FULL run never trips the\ndiscriminator, so it would still die budget-exhausted on genuine breadth — and per the stated intent\nthat is the correct outcome, not a bug to paper over. What the fix does deliver:\n\n* the three pathological runs above would have been cut at calls 88 / 151 / 196 and **landed a\n  verdict** on the evidence gathered, instead of being killed empty with `--force-close` as the only\n  exit;\n* 40-70% of each pathological run's spend is reclaimed;\n* the failure mode the budget was introduced for is bounded for the first time.\n\nA path to e369 reaching a verdict exists — **AC split PLUS the actuator** (each half's genuine work,\n76 and 167 distinct calls, sits inside the budget once its loop is cut) — but that is two tickets, and\nneither promises a **PASS**. Criterion #1's Gerrit half remains unreachable by any repo tool\n([rebar:6ec8-24c7-182b-40d0]), and the flat budget still does not scale with claim volume\n([rebar:a89d-4dd2-f421-417a], [rebar:d59e-e042-2b94-4b70]).\n\n## Dependency\n\nThis ticket's actuator needs a discriminator that separates cycles from breadth, so it is **blocked on\n[rebar:70bc-ab05-6d4c-45a0]** (the `max_consecutive_repeat` blind spot). Wiring an actuator to\ntoday's metric would fire on healthy runs and miss real cycles — the exact inversion measured above.\n\nEvidence: `/tmp/c827-trace.jsonl` (FULL, 258), `/tmp/c827-split-A.jsonl` (257),\n`/tmp/c827-split-B.jsonl` (270), `/tmp/bf31-trace-evidence.jsonl` (264), and the run logs beside them.\ne369 was NOT modified — it remains `in_progress`. Nothing was closed or force-closed."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785945030738110001,"uuid":"4dc89397-c383-455c-a0cb-eb178ec52470"}